                <path d="M12 8h.01"></path>
              </svg>
            </span>
            <div class="note-card-body">
              <span class="note-card-title">Note</span>
              <p class="note-card-text">{ex.note}</p>
            </div>
          </aside>
        </article>
      ))}

      <!--
        `.note-card` here for the same reason as the per-example notes above, and
        for one more: this is the conclusion the whole section exists to deliver.
        The dashed left rule stayed reserved for the computed lines (the equation
        strip and the merit-score bar), so "this was computed" and "this is what
        to remember" now look different.
      -->
      <aside class="note-card mt-12">
        <span class="note-card-icon" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <path d="M9 18h6"></path>
            <path d="M10 22h4"></path>
            <path d="M15.09 14c.18-.98.65-1.74 1.41-2.5A4.65 4.65 0 0 0 18 8 6 6 0 0 0 6 8c0 1 .23 2.23 1.5 3.5A4.61 4.61 0 0 1 8.91 14"></path>
          </svg>
        </span>
        <div class="note-card-body">
          <span class="note-card-title">The takeaway</span>
          <p class="note-card-text">
            <strong class="font-semibold text-slate-900">Read across the four examples and the pattern is obvious:</strong>
            identical board marks produced 166.5, 73.61, 65.25 and 454 across four states. None of those numbers is
            wrong. They are measured on four different scales, and only one of them belongs to your admission.
            That is the entire argument for using a state level cut off calculator instead of a generic one, and
            for checking the conducting authority on your scorecard before you calculate anything.
          </p>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
